1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,860
...triod of Pisa, more dangerous today than ever before.

2
00:00:04,660 --> 00:00:08,100
The dream of unlimited nuclear power turns deadly.

3
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:13,100
Three cosmonauts pay the ultimate price in the space race.

4
00:00:14,060 --> 00:00:16,640
The U.S. Air Force meets a tragic end at sea.

5
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:22,520
And NASA loses its way and millions of dollars on the trip to Mars.

6
00:00:23,080 --> 00:00:27,060
Now, Engineering Disasters 3 on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
With this building, nine centuries old, began the saga of one of the most visible and enduring

8
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:47,660
engineering disasters the world has ever known.

9
00:00:49,800 --> 00:00:55,320
In 1063, construction began on a cathedral in the Italian city of Pisa.

10
00:00:56,560 --> 00:00:59,520
It was the largest Romanesque church in Tuscany.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:09,220
The Duomo, as it was known, was the centerpiece of a group of buildings that included a baptistry and a bell tower.

12
00:01:11,180 --> 00:01:21,560
In August of 1171, workers dug the foundation for the tower, which was to soar 183 feet into the sky, the tallest in the region.

13
00:01:21,561 --> 00:01:26,560
At that time, many towns were competing for towers.

14
00:01:26,561 --> 00:01:33,560
Especially in Italy, there were towers built in Pisa, there were towers built in Bologna, in Rome.

15
00:01:33,561 --> 00:01:36,560
Each family tried to build one bigger than the next one.

16
00:01:36,561 --> 00:01:40,560
Towers at the time were considered to be status symbols.

17
00:01:40,561 --> 00:01:48,560
The first three stories, the tall ground story, and the first two logias, or open galleries,

18
00:01:48,561 --> 00:01:53,008
galleries were built between 1173 and 1178.

19
00:01:53,009 --> 00:01:57,560
The next four logias were added between 1272

20
00:01:57,561 --> 00:01:58,831
and 1278.

21
00:01:58,832 --> 00:02:09,560
While adding the next logias, the builders discovered that the tower's great

22
00:02:09,561 --> 00:02:16,560
height and consequent weight of 14,000 tons caused it to sink unevenly into the ground.

23
00:02:16,561 --> 00:02:29,198
Where the tower sits is not perfectly flat, not perfectly even, so the high side, what started off as the high side of the tower, seemed to be on sturdier soil than

24
00:02:29,199 --> 00:02:30,200
the low side.

25
00:02:30,201 --> 00:02:32,960
So the low side kept sinking further and further down.

26
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:41,380
Undaunted, the builders believed that they could correct the lean by building the fourth story taller on the south side.

27
00:02:41,381 --> 00:02:47,560
But the extra weight of the new construction made the tower tilt farther still.

28
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:53,000
By 1298, the tower was leaning more than four and a half feet.

29
00:02:54,180 --> 00:02:56,820
The belfry was added in 1370.

30
00:02:57,940 --> 00:03:02,980
By 1550, the tower's lean increased to about 12 and a half feet.

31
00:03:03,940 --> 00:03:10,060
Until the early 20th century, very little was known about the properties of soil.

32
00:03:10,061 --> 00:03:14,340
It was thought of merely if you put your building on the ground and you try to put it on rock.

33
00:03:14,660 --> 00:03:17,700
If you can't put it on rock, you spread the load out as much as you can.

34
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:22,820
Odd as it was, this regional landmark could not be abandoned.

35
00:03:23,980 --> 00:03:28,620
In 1911, the Italian government began taking careful measurements each year.

36
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:37,100
Engineers discovered, to their dismay, that the tower was tipping southward at the rate of 1 20th of an inch per year.

37
00:03:37,101 --> 00:03:46,100
From the time it was completed in 1370, the tower had leaned more than 13 feet off the vertical.

38
00:03:46,101 --> 00:03:51,100
Engineers continued to search for solutions.

39
00:03:51,101 --> 00:03:57,100
In 1934, they hit upon the idea of stabilizing the tower's uncertain foundation

40
00:03:57,101 --> 00:04:05,100
by drilling 361 holes around the base and injecting 80 tons of grout to firm up the soil.

41
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:08,660
Their efforts were for naught.

42
00:04:09,380 --> 00:04:11,580
Rather than strengthening the ground beneath the tower,

43
00:04:12,180 --> 00:04:16,660
the holes made the ground less stable, and the tower's lean increased.

44
00:04:17,900 --> 00:04:21,660
The Tower of Pisa is a lesson for engineers and architects,

45
00:04:21,860 --> 00:04:26,460
and that is that you have to be very careful what you're building upon,

46
00:04:26,461 --> 00:04:33,300
because what you're building on may actually end up being what causes the problem in your structure.

47
00:04:33,301 --> 00:04:42,040
That is why today we are very concerned and we explore the soils under a building long before the building is started.

48
00:04:43,960 --> 00:04:51,020
Experts acknowledge that an earthquake, a windstorm, or even a heavy rain might send the tower crashing to earth.

49
00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:54,440
It was closed to the public in 1990.

50
00:04:54,441 --> 00:05:05,860
A fresh approach was needed, and engineers decided that better data might help them address the city's very public engineering fiasco.

51
00:05:07,780 --> 00:05:15,480
They embarked on the ambitious project of wiring the tower with 120 sensors to monitor its every inclination.

52
00:05:16,540 --> 00:05:21,860
They learned that in the late morning, the tower leaned away from the sun to the northwest,

53
00:05:21,861 --> 00:05:26,307
west, as its southeastern side warmed up and expanded.

54
00:05:26,308 --> 00:05:28,860
At night, the southeastern side

55
00:05:28,861 --> 00:05:32,405
cooled down, and the tower leaned back.

56
00:05:32,406 --> 00:05:36,860
By studying the soft clay beneath the foundation,

57
00:05:36,861 --> 00:05:40,860
architectural historians determined that the centuries-long delays during construction

58
00:05:40,861 --> 00:05:45,604
could provide a clue to setting the tower straight.

59
00:05:45,605 --> 00:05:48,860
The delays allowed the clay beneath

60
00:05:48,861 --> 00:05:51,971
the tower to settle and become stronger.

61
00:05:51,972 --> 00:05:55,860
Without those delays, historians believe the tower

62
00:05:56,300 --> 00:05:58,407
would have toppled long ago.

63
00:05:58,408 --> 00:06:02,620
This seemed to confirm the idea that the problem lay not

64
00:06:02,621 --> 00:06:08,620
with the tower, but with the ground upon which it stood.

65
00:06:08,621 --> 00:06:15,620
In June of 1999, engineers discovered a way to steer the tower by changing the soil composition

66
00:06:15,621 --> 00:06:18,860
beneath it.

67
00:06:18,861 --> 00:06:23,860
Forty-one drill pipes were arrayed around the north quadrant of the tower.

68
00:06:23,861 --> 00:06:28,860
They entered the soil at different points along an arc about forty feet from the base,

69
00:06:28,861 --> 00:06:31,860
at an angle of thirty degrees.

70
00:06:31,861 --> 00:06:35,860
Inside each eight-inch diameter pipe was an auger,

71
00:06:35,861 --> 00:06:42,860
a corkscrew bit that trapped soil between its blades and channeled it to the surface.

72
00:06:42,861 --> 00:06:47,860
The tower settled into the yard-long channels created by the bits.

73
00:06:47,861 --> 00:06:53,860
Perhaps this last attempt will actually prevent the tower from leaning any further.

74
00:06:54,860 --> 00:07:02,860
By the end of summer 1999, the tower had tilted back toward the north by about one inch and a half at the top.

75
00:07:03,860 --> 00:07:07,860
Engineers expect to auger more soil from beneath the tower's high side

76
00:07:08,860 --> 00:07:14,860
and return the structure to the five degree lean it last saw in the 19th century.

77
00:07:14,861 --> 00:07:23,500
It's clear that once the tower does not lean anymore, the Pizans are not going to want to straighten the tower out,

78
00:07:23,501 --> 00:07:26,120
because, after all, who would want to see a straight tower of Pisa?

79
00:07:27,480 --> 00:07:30,880
Some estimates put the cost of saving the tower at $7 million.

80
00:07:32,480 --> 00:07:38,520
But its saviors say it's worth it to have a safer, if not completely straight, Tower of Pisa.

81
00:07:38,521 --> 00:07:45,520
The tower's faults have made it one of the world's major tourist attractions.

82
00:07:45,521 --> 00:07:50,520
But in the lonely high desert of the American Northwest,

83
00:07:50,521 --> 00:07:53,520
there was a vastly different engineering disaster.

84
00:07:53,521 --> 00:07:57,520
So terrifying, it was buried beneath the earth.

85
00:07:57,521 --> 00:08:05,520
In January 1961, three men would die of injuries received here.

86
00:08:05,521 --> 00:08:10,140
the site of the world's first peacetime nuclear accident.

87
00:08:12,140 --> 00:08:17,100
Splitting the atom revealed all that was great and horrible about human intelligence.

88
00:08:17,980 --> 00:08:23,140
It was the final word in settling World War II, a constant threat during the Cold War,

89
00:08:23,720 --> 00:08:28,880
and, its proponents believed, a grand opportunity to power the world peacefully.

90
00:08:28,881 --> 00:08:30,857
In 1958, the U.S.

91
00:08:30,858 --> 00:08:38,880
Army started construction on the SL-1, the Station Low Power Reactor,

92
00:08:38,881 --> 00:08:42,880
at the Nuclear Reactor Testing Facility in Southeastern Idaho.

93
00:08:42,881 --> 00:08:49,880
It was used as a training site for future deployments of this reactor type.

94
00:08:49,881 --> 00:08:53,880
It would take about six months for an operator to be fully trained.

95
00:08:53,881 --> 00:08:54,957
fully trained.

96
00:08:54,958 --> 00:09:00,880
The reactor ran without incident for two and a half years, only shutting down

97
00:09:00,881 --> 00:09:04,260
for maintenance over the Christmas holiday.

98
00:09:04,261 --> 00:09:07,640
After the new year, a three-man maintenance

99
00:09:07,641 --> 00:09:10,699
crew was assigned to restart the reactor.

100
00:09:10,700 --> 00:09:14,280
The routine of operating and testing the reactor

101
00:09:14,281 --> 00:09:22,780
was to operate it for any number of days or weeks, shut it down, train the crew in maintaining

102
00:09:22,781 --> 00:09:27,125
the reactor, maintenance tasks, possibly make changes.

103
00:09:27,126 --> 00:09:29,780
It operated in this fashion while

104
00:09:29,781 --> 00:09:36,620
the trainees were certified and given the instruction that they needed.

105
00:09:36,621 --> 00:09:42,493
The core of uranium-235 gave off subatomic particles called neutrons.

106
00:09:42,494 --> 00:09:43,940
It was surrounded

107
00:09:43,941 --> 00:09:49,060
by water which cooled the system and slowed the neutrons to a rate suitable for fission

108
00:09:49,061 --> 00:09:49,907
to occur.

109
00:09:49,908 --> 00:09:57,620
Inside the 13-ton reactor vessel, control rods made of boron regulated the fission

110
00:09:57,621 --> 00:10:02,784
reaction. The rods absorbed the neutrons produced by the core.

111
00:10:02,785 --> 00:10:05,200
Therefore, when the rods were

112
00:10:05,201 --> 00:10:11,369
lowered into the core area and absorbed neutrons, the fission slowed down.

113
00:10:11,370 --> 00:10:13,120
When they were raised

114
00:10:13,121 --> 00:10:18,020
and the neutrons flowed freely, the fission reactions sped up.

115
00:10:18,021 --> 00:10:24,660
So if you want to regulate the rate at which the neutron reaction is going,

116
00:10:24,661 --> 00:10:31,020
you regulate the control rods in the reactor itself.

117
00:10:31,021 --> 00:10:38,080
At 9 a.m. on January 3, 1961, an alarm rang out at the fire station.

118
00:10:38,081 --> 00:10:43,000
The trouble spot was designated ARA, Army Reactor Area.

119
00:10:44,360 --> 00:10:48,380
It turned out to be a false alarm triggered by a bulky heat detector.

120
00:10:49,560 --> 00:10:56,120
The crew working to restart the reactor heard another fire alarm at 4 p.m., also false.

121
00:10:57,400 --> 00:11:02,320
But at 9 p.m., a fateful third alarm signal rang out.

122
00:11:02,321 --> 00:11:10,180
Six Atomic Energy Commission firefighters, an assistant chief and a security officer

123
00:11:10,181 --> 00:11:13,380
rolled to the reactor site 12 miles away.

124
00:11:14,160 --> 00:11:17,360
Now it is 17 below zero.

125
00:11:18,060 --> 00:11:24,740
We've been out there twice during the day already, and so we're human,

126
00:11:24,980 --> 00:11:28,020
and we're disgruntled because we have to get out in the night,

127
00:11:28,160 --> 00:11:30,680
and we have to go back out in this cold.

128
00:11:30,681 --> 00:11:37,680
They arrived nine minutes later to find nothing out of the ordinary.

129
00:11:37,681 --> 00:11:44,420
There was no bulky heat indicator this time, and certainly no sign of fire.

130
00:11:44,421 --> 00:11:51,680
But once inside the SL1 building, they saw the flashing indicator of a radiation alarm.

131
00:11:51,681 --> 00:11:53,680
The area was compromised.

132
00:11:53,681 --> 00:11:56,680
A radioactive leak, or worse.

133
00:11:56,681 --> 00:12:00,440
And strangely, the reactor maintenance crew was nowhere to be found,

134
00:12:00,860 --> 00:12:06,140
even though their coats hung on the rack and their lunch pails were in the cafeteria.

135
00:12:07,240 --> 00:12:10,100
That plant was not run with zero people.

136
00:12:11,120 --> 00:12:16,820
Could it have been that when something happened, maybe they took off across the desert,

137
00:12:17,140 --> 00:12:19,600
evacuated the plant? We did not know that.

138
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:25,060
As the firefighters approached the stairs to the second floor reactor room,

139
00:12:25,820 --> 00:12:27,960
They noticed a new disturbing development.

140
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:33,280
The instruments they used to measure radiation pegged at their maximum.

141
00:12:34,700 --> 00:12:40,680
The firefighters left the building, but the assistant fire chief, concerned about the maintenance crew,

142
00:12:41,340 --> 00:12:46,040
continued on upstairs, alone, to check the reactor room.

143
00:12:47,300 --> 00:12:51,640
There he made a grisly discovery, a body motionless on the floor.

144
00:12:51,641 --> 00:12:58,640
We were told that we should not enter the complex again because of the hazard of the high radiation.

145
00:12:58,641 --> 00:13:05,640
A fresh team arrived with robust instruments capable of measuring high radiation levels.

146
00:13:05,641 --> 00:13:13,640
Inside the SL-1, their instruments also pegged at their maximum, 500 Röntgens.

147
00:13:13,641 --> 00:13:18,640
Typically, a single dose of 600 Röntgens is fatal.

148
00:13:18,641 --> 00:13:29,080
Next, a search and rescue team risks their lives to go inside the SL-1 and investigate the disaster.

149
00:13:31,300 --> 00:13:35,720
There are 430 operational nuclear reactors worldwide.

150
00:13:36,440 --> 00:13:39,700
They provide 16% of the world's electricity.

151
00:13:40,980 --> 00:13:44,340
Engineering Disasters 3 will return on Modern Marvels.

152
00:13:44,341 --> 00:13:53,411
In January 1961, the world's first peacetime nuclear accident presented troubling issues for the U.S.

153
00:13:53,412 --> 00:13:54,112
Army.

154
00:13:55,700 --> 00:13:58,260
The entire reactor was a hot zone.

155
00:13:59,680 --> 00:14:04,900
At 10.30 p.m., five technicians in full protective gear entered the contaminated area.

156
00:14:06,300 --> 00:14:11,640
Each man carried at least one film badge, which recorded his radiation exposure.

157
00:14:11,641 --> 00:14:18,640
Just as photographic film reacts to light energy, it is also affected by radioactive energy.

158
00:14:18,641 --> 00:14:25,640
The badges were examined to be sure no man received a radiation dose beyond the acceptable limit.

159
00:14:25,641 --> 00:14:32,640
A technician called a health physicist was on site to watch over the process.

160
00:14:32,641 --> 00:14:39,640
Because of the extreme health risk, he allowed each team of rescue workers in the reactor room for only one minute.

161
00:14:39,641 --> 00:14:40,341
minute.

162
00:14:40,342 --> 00:14:45,760
Someone told me he had a big metal wrench of some kind and when your minute was up,

163
00:14:46,200 --> 00:14:53,080
he'd bang on the wall and you'd rush out of there with your one minute of work done.

164
00:14:54,080 --> 00:14:58,300
The men inside the reactor building presented their own radiation hazards

165
00:14:58,301 --> 00:15:04,380
and technicians took special precautions when handling them to prevent the spread of contamination.

166
00:15:04,381 --> 00:15:12,380
One victim who was clinging to life was transported to a remote desert location for a medical exam.

167
00:15:13,380 --> 00:15:17,640
The site doctor immediately responded out across the desert.

168
00:15:17,780 --> 00:15:20,000
It probably took him a half an hour to get there.

169
00:15:20,220 --> 00:15:22,000
This was quite a driving distance.

170
00:15:22,160 --> 00:15:26,060
The doctor checked him over and then declared he had expired

171
00:15:26,061 --> 00:15:29,340
because we don't want to spread contamination all over the desert.

172
00:15:29,341 --> 00:15:34,740
The ambulance sat there for quite a while until they decided for sure what they wanted to do at that point.

173
00:15:35,500 --> 00:15:39,920
Later that night, the last guy was found in an improbable place.

174
00:15:41,420 --> 00:15:48,060
No one had seen him because he was pinned to the ceiling of the reactor room, impaled by a control rod.

175
00:15:50,880 --> 00:15:55,420
A photographer was given permission to suit up, go in and take photographs

176
00:15:55,421 --> 00:16:00,320
because they had to figure out how they were going to remove the body of the third man pinned up on the ceiling.

177
00:16:00,680 --> 00:16:03,640
They didn't know whether the reactor was stable or not.

178
00:16:03,880 --> 00:16:05,840
They didn't know if it could go critical again.

179
00:16:06,300 --> 00:16:09,680
They certainly didn't want anything falling into one of those openings.

180
00:16:10,740 --> 00:16:15,000
Technicians made a sling to lift him out without disturbing the sensitive hot zone.

181
00:16:15,880 --> 00:16:20,820
It would be hard to recover the idea of how much shock this produced.

182
00:16:20,821 --> 00:16:28,800
The entire history of the Atomic Energy Commission, 16 years, had never generated an accident like this.

183
00:16:29,460 --> 00:16:30,600
I mean, it was unprecedented.

184
00:16:31,680 --> 00:16:38,320
General Electric, experienced in nuclear decontamination, was privately contracted to dismantle and secure the site.

185
00:16:40,520 --> 00:16:47,240
As spring 1961 turned to summer, the reactor building was broken down piece by piece.

186
00:16:47,241 --> 00:16:55,340
Some sections of the SL1 were buried nearby after passing a radiation check.

187
00:16:56,820 --> 00:17:05,780
Others, which could have held the secret to the disaster, were transported 40 miles in sealed vehicles to a facility called the Hot Shop.

188
00:17:06,780 --> 00:17:10,000
There, they would undergo decontamination and analysis.

189
00:17:11,360 --> 00:17:14,540
This painstaking work continued for 11 months.

190
00:17:14,541 --> 00:17:23,740
Then, finally, a crane lifted the entire reactor vessel from the gutted building and loaded it onto a vehicle for transport to the hot shop.

191
00:17:25,740 --> 00:17:31,980
The difficult job of dismantling the reactor was complete, but the real work was just beginning.

192
00:17:33,780 --> 00:17:37,760
In the hot shop, the reactor vessel was picked over for clues.

193
00:17:38,700 --> 00:17:44,060
Analyzing scratch marks along it, investigators determined that uncontrolled nuclear fission,

194
00:17:44,540 --> 00:17:51,320
called an excursion, generated great heat, causing water surrounding the core to turn into high-pressure steam.

195
00:17:52,460 --> 00:17:57,100
This pressurized steam launched the reactor vessel upward like a rocket.

196
00:17:57,660 --> 00:18:04,700
It all happened so fast that the steam forced the water up, hit the top of the reactor at 10,000 pounds per square inch.

197
00:18:04,701 --> 00:18:05,887
terrific force.

198
00:18:05,888 --> 00:18:11,980
And the reactor actually jumped nine feet into the air and was stopped by the

199
00:18:11,981 --> 00:18:18,020
ceiling of the room it was in. There are no survivors of this accident. There was no

200
00:18:18,021 --> 00:18:20,001
testimony, no witnesses.

201
00:18:20,002 --> 00:18:25,200
But the evidence found that this central control rod had indeed

202
00:18:25,201 --> 00:18:29,601
been moved up to 20 inches. That was a design flaw.

203
00:18:29,602 --> 00:18:32,880
Investigators believe that the reactor

204
00:18:32,881 --> 00:18:38,680
went critical because one of its control rods was pulled out past the safe limit of 4.2 inches.

205
00:18:40,660 --> 00:18:45,920
Since the control rods regulate the fission reaction, removing one or more would cause

206
00:18:45,921 --> 00:18:53,360
the nuclear reaction to accelerate out of control. What remained a mystery was why a

207
00:18:53,361 --> 00:18:59,300
trained worker would pull the rod out so far, knowing full well the dire consequences of

208
00:18:59,301 --> 00:19:02,060
doing so. Theories abounded.

209
00:19:02,061 --> 00:19:07,580
Most convincing was that the control rod simply stuck in

210
00:19:07,581 --> 00:19:08,627
its housing.

211
00:19:08,628 --> 00:19:15,080
It required so much force to move that it was inadvertently jerked upward.

212
00:19:15,081 --> 00:19:21,180
SL1 plant records showed that indeed the rods had a history of sticking and at times were

213
00:19:21,181 --> 00:19:27,280
difficult to relocate, even during the simple maintenance the crew was attempting to perform.

214
00:19:28,980 --> 00:19:33,800
This business of having stuck control rods the previous month or two never should have

215
00:19:33,801 --> 00:19:37,316
been permitted to go on unless an explanation had been found for that.

216
00:19:37,317 --> 00:19:38,220
Something had been

217
00:19:38,221 --> 00:19:43,291
lacking in the attention to detail on safe operation of this reactor.

218
00:19:43,292 --> 00:19:44,540
The investigation

219
00:19:44,541 --> 00:19:51,040
complete, the SL-1 program was scrapped, and the Atomic Energy Commission was plunged into

220
00:19:51,041 --> 00:19:56,040
to a morass of official accusation, investigation, and self-examination.

221
00:19:56,041 --> 00:19:59,040
They wanted the rest of the laboratories to learn from this.

222
00:19:59,041 --> 00:20:04,040
Some of the things they learned were applied after the Three Mile Island accident,

223
00:20:04,041 --> 00:20:09,040
the cleanup methods, the management of equipment, the management of people, the protection of people.

224
00:20:09,041 --> 00:20:14,040
The people at this site made it their business to consider this matter,

225
00:20:14,041 --> 00:20:20,040
and so they designed certain techniques and tools and procedures should it ever happen again.

226
00:20:20,041 --> 00:20:22,040
Thank God it hasn't.

227
00:20:22,041 --> 00:20:30,040
Since the SL1, no reactor has been designed to go critical when the central control rod is removed.

228
00:20:30,041 --> 00:20:34,040
All human interaction has been eliminated.

229
00:20:34,041 --> 00:20:41,040
Computer-controlled mechanisms move the rods, and they cannot be pulled past the critical point at any time.

230
00:20:41,041 --> 00:20:49,040
While the nuclear power industry worked hard to address the technical issues brought up by the SL1 disaster,

231
00:20:49,041 --> 00:20:56,040
the psychological damage had been done, and the promise of nuclear power would never shine as brightly again.

232
00:21:00,040 --> 00:21:07,040
Next, the defenders of the free world build a radar tower that proves to be a greater enemy than the Soviets.

233
00:21:10,040 --> 00:21:11,242
In 1954, the U.S.

234
00:21:11,243 --> 00:21:17,040
government was carefully monitoring a disturbing buildup of Soviet military might.

235
00:21:19,040 --> 00:21:26,580
In the days before the intercontinental ballistic missile, long-range Soviet bombers had America in their crosshairs.

236
00:21:29,200 --> 00:21:35,500
American fighter jets were an effective countermeasure, but their radar range was only 15 miles.

237
00:21:36,680 --> 00:21:37,888
Therefore, the U.S.

238
00:21:37,889 --> 00:21:44,180
military set up a radar early warning system to detect any aircraft that entered American airspace.

239
00:21:44,181 --> 00:21:50,960
At the farthest reaches of the defensive line were RC-121 reconnaissance aircraft.

240
00:21:52,640 --> 00:21:56,280
Next were radar-equipped seagoing vessels called picket ships.

241
00:21:58,080 --> 00:22:03,380
Any report of enemy contact was to be radioed to the next planned link in the defensive chain,

242
00:22:04,040 --> 00:22:07,340
a group of five offshore radar installations.

243
00:22:07,341 --> 00:22:14,340
These artificial islands were called Texas Towers because of their design similarities

244
00:22:14,720 --> 00:22:17,536
to oil rigs off the coast of Texas.

245
00:22:17,537 --> 00:22:21,720
Of the planned Quintet, three towers were built from

246
00:22:21,721 --> 00:22:23,038
1957 until 1960.

247
00:22:23,039 --> 00:22:28,720
Tower 2 was constructed with its legs attached to the radar platform,

248
00:22:28,721 --> 00:22:35,720
then floated to its position 110 miles off the coast of Massachusetts in 56 feet of water.

249
00:22:37,340 --> 00:22:42,760
Tower 3, constructed similarly, was positioned nearby in 80 feet of water.

250
00:22:44,400 --> 00:22:52,860
Tower 4, to be placed 78 miles off the coast of New Jersey, was to be in the deepest water, 185 feet.

251
00:22:53,520 --> 00:22:56,500
Tower 2 and 3 were built with 15 legs.

252
00:22:56,780 --> 00:23:03,380
They had three permanent and 12 that they jettisoned off after they had it up.

253
00:23:03,381 --> 00:23:09,560
Tower 4 went out with two legs on the water and one above, all braced together on a pontoon.

254
00:23:10,280 --> 00:23:15,020
Perhaps because it had longer legs than the others, Tower 4 was constructed differently.

255
00:23:15,860 --> 00:23:20,100
The leg assembly, seen here as it was floated separately from the tower platform,

256
00:23:20,780 --> 00:23:27,020
encountered rough weather on the 360-mile trip from the North Portland main shipyard where it was built.

257
00:23:27,021 --> 00:23:37,180
On the way to its position off the coast of New Jersey, at least two braces fell from the legs, weakening the structure.

258
00:23:38,800 --> 00:23:45,840
Though the Air Force brass were aware of the damage, they elected to make repairs after the legs were connected to the base.

259
00:23:46,940 --> 00:23:50,177
I think the mistake was they repaired it after it was up.

260
00:23:50,178 --> 00:23:53,300
I think that was the most critical time for that tower.

261
00:23:54,040 --> 00:23:59,640
After nine months under construction, the bill for Tower 4 came to $21 million.

262
00:24:00,460 --> 00:24:05,700
It was big enough for a six-story building to fit below its triangular steel platform.

263
00:24:08,880 --> 00:24:12,240
The Air Force assigned 70 men to serve on the tower.

264
00:24:13,520 --> 00:24:20,200
A mix of military radar technicians and civilians who maintained and repaired the massive man-made island.

265
00:24:20,201 --> 00:24:27,200
My dad got a job as a welder on Tower Number 4. I was a refueler.

266
00:24:27,201 --> 00:24:33,200
Don Abbott refueled Boeing H-21 helicopters for the trip out to Tower 4.

267
00:24:33,201 --> 00:24:37,200
His father, David, was often a passenger.

268
00:24:37,201 --> 00:24:44,200
Life was hard and cold aboard the tower, but the Air Force did its best to make it bearable.

269
00:24:44,201 --> 00:24:48,300
When one crew was working, the other crew was playing.

270
00:24:48,740 --> 00:24:53,220
We had pool tables, ping pong tables, and we had fishing.

271
00:24:53,480 --> 00:24:54,560
We even had basketball.

272
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:57,240
The only problem was there was only one ball.

273
00:24:57,560 --> 00:25:00,460
If the ball went overboard, they had to go and get it.

274
00:25:00,820 --> 00:25:05,100
They did all their cooking on the tower, had fresh rolls every day.

275
00:25:06,300 --> 00:25:09,320
The amenities helped the crew cope with rough conditions.

276
00:25:10,280 --> 00:25:14,720
But from day one, Tower 4 shook more violently than the others.

277
00:25:15,500 --> 00:25:19,740
And over its next two years in service, it was lashed by two hurricanes.

278
00:25:21,500 --> 00:25:29,420
I had a plum in my cabin, and I used to watch it go back and forth, or it even turned, it gyrates.

279
00:25:29,800 --> 00:25:33,200
That was my ticket of knowing what was going on with the tower.

280
00:25:33,840 --> 00:25:37,540
They decided that they were going to name it Old Shaky, and that's what they did.

281
00:25:39,320 --> 00:25:43,520
The men who served on Tower 4 knew they were taking a risk.

282
00:25:45,060 --> 00:25:49,080
But for Lieutenant Colonel Cutler, safety was a top priority.

283
00:25:50,040 --> 00:25:52,960
He notified headquarters about the tower's gyration.

284
00:25:53,220 --> 00:25:56,160
I sent a letter in saying that we have a problem here.

285
00:25:56,360 --> 00:25:59,400
We have to send a crew out here to try to do something with it.

286
00:26:00,380 --> 00:26:04,120
Divers were sent into the water to attach K-braces to the tower legs.

287
00:26:05,420 --> 00:26:06,960
And it didn't last that long.

288
00:26:07,160 --> 00:26:08,180
And that didn't do the job.

289
00:26:08,181 --> 00:26:11,226
That told me one thing. Everything's going backwards.

290
00:26:11,227 --> 00:26:14,100
Nothing's going forwards, no matter what they did.

291
00:26:15,200 --> 00:26:21,300
By spring of 1960, Lieutenant Colonel Cutler completed his one-year tour of duty on the tower.

292
00:26:22,020 --> 00:26:25,940
He was relieved by a career Navy man, Captain Gordon Phelan.

293
00:26:26,860 --> 00:26:33,660
By fall of 1960, the shaking was so severe that most of the men serving on Tower 4 were evacuated,

294
00:26:33,661 --> 00:26:39,340
leaving a skeleton crew of 14 servicemen and 14 civilian repairmen.

295
00:26:41,620 --> 00:26:48,220
On the evening of January 14, 1961, a severe storm closed in on the tower.

296
00:26:49,080 --> 00:26:53,560
By dawn, 55 mile per hour winds were beating the ocean into a frenzy.

297
00:26:54,160 --> 00:26:59,160
That day, Mrs. Fennel, Gordon's wife, had spoken to her husband twice.

298
00:26:59,300 --> 00:27:03,440
Once around 1 o'clock and the second time at 6 o'clock.

299
00:27:03,660 --> 00:27:08,840
During that one o'clock conversation, she could hear noises in the background.

300
00:27:09,380 --> 00:27:13,140
She asked what that was, and he said, well, the tower's breaking up.

301
00:27:13,380 --> 00:27:18,940
In a macabre type of humor, there was a lot of metal, banging against metal.

302
00:27:19,500 --> 00:27:22,860
One of the support beams on the leg had broken loose

303
00:27:22,861 --> 00:27:27,280
and was just literally swinging back and forth against the leg.

304
00:27:28,220 --> 00:27:33,080
As the storm continued to mount its assault, official indecision reigned.

305
00:27:33,660 --> 00:27:40,900
In the Air Force chain of command, no one was certain who had the authority to abandon a tower so critical to America's national defense.

306
00:27:42,800 --> 00:27:49,780
By 4 p.m., Captain Phelan took matters into his own hands and called for helicopters to evacuate Tower 4.

307
00:27:51,120 --> 00:27:55,020
Seas were so high, no rescue ship could dare approach.

308
00:27:55,021 --> 00:28:09,021
We had a 5,000-ton structure sitting on top of three 12-and-a-half diameter thin-walled legs 67 feet above the surface of the ocean, being slammed by waves higher than

309
00:28:09,233 --> 00:28:14,340
a three-story building, winds gusting over 55 miles an hour.

310
00:28:14,980 --> 00:28:20,760
There was no way at that time that you could lower anybody to a ship standing by.

311
00:28:20,761 --> 00:28:29,680
At 7.30 p.m., the tower's glowing image vanished from the radar screens of all ships in the area.

312
00:28:30,880 --> 00:28:34,880
The captain was watching his radar and couldn't believe it.

313
00:28:34,980 --> 00:28:39,660
He went to his backup radar unit, checked that, tower wasn't there.

314
00:28:41,040 --> 00:28:46,620
He grabbed the radio and shouted, Tower 4, Tower 4, no response.

315
00:28:46,621 --> 00:28:50,080
He flipped over to the emergency broadcast channel.

316
00:28:50,520 --> 00:28:53,620
Again, he cried, Tower 4, Tower 4.

317
00:28:54,140 --> 00:28:56,040
Nothing but static came back.

318
00:28:57,160 --> 00:29:00,400
At 8 p.m., rescue workers reached the site.

319
00:29:01,400 --> 00:29:03,760
They found only debris and heaving seas.

320
00:29:04,440 --> 00:29:05,920
Tower 4 was gone.

321
00:29:06,940 --> 00:29:11,520
Only one body was eventually recovered, that of Master Sergeant Ronald Backey.

322
00:29:11,521 --> 00:29:16,740
Donald Abbott's father was among the men missing and presumed dead

323
00:29:16,741 --> 00:29:19,000
The phone rang at our house

324
00:29:19,001 --> 00:29:23,660
and at the same time we heard a thump at the front door

325
00:29:23,661 --> 00:29:26,500
It was a young fellow delivering the Boston Globe

326
00:29:26,501 --> 00:29:28,140
They just had it all over the front page

327
00:29:28,141 --> 00:29:29,880
We had no warning whatsoever

328
00:29:29,881 --> 00:29:34,920
and my aunt was on the telephone telling my mom that the tower had collapsed

329
00:29:34,921 --> 00:29:36,260
and that all 28 were dead

330
00:29:36,261 --> 00:29:42,080
I didn't see anything on the tower that was worth losing 28 people.

331
00:29:42,340 --> 00:29:44,980
And I say that as a USAF officer.

332
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:52,060
Congress assailed the Air Force for its mistakes in the design and maintenance of Tower 4.

333
00:29:53,960 --> 00:29:57,860
Pinned connections were too weak to withstand unpredictable sea conditions.

334
00:29:59,020 --> 00:30:02,380
Structural deficiencies in the tower legs should have been addressed

335
00:30:02,381 --> 00:30:05,820
before they were magnified by the action of wind and waves.

336
00:30:06,260 --> 00:30:11,040
What they did for Tower 4 had never been done before.

337
00:30:11,240 --> 00:30:13,760
This was an engineering milestone.

338
00:30:14,140 --> 00:30:18,720
I'm sure it was designed to the best of their knowledge at that time.

339
00:30:19,580 --> 00:30:24,860
Today, Tower 4 is a wreck 185 feet below the surface of the Atlantic.

340
00:30:25,480 --> 00:30:31,760
A silent witness to a brave experiment in national defense that went very wrong.

341
00:30:31,761 --> 00:30:45,720
On July 14, 1999, divers placed a plaque on Tower 4, honoring the 28 men who gave their lives, serving on an artificial island at sea.

342
00:30:49,220 --> 00:30:54,000
Next, a triumphant Soviet space mission has an unexpected ending.

343
00:30:54,001 --> 00:31:04,480
The last Texas tower was dismantled in 1963, made obsolete by AWACS, the Airborne Warning and Control System.

344
00:31:05,200 --> 00:31:09,040
Engineering Disasters 3 will return on Modern Marvels.

345
00:31:11,360 --> 00:31:16,840
June 29, 1971, dawned a proud day for the Soviet space program.

346
00:31:16,841 --> 00:31:25,040
The three cosmonauts of Soyuz 11 had lived in space aboard Salyut 1 for more than three weeks.

347
00:31:26,540 --> 00:31:30,460
In the Soviet view, it was the crowning achievement of the space race.

348
00:31:31,260 --> 00:31:38,080
Well, this entire era was about the competition in the space race, which was a critical part of the Cold War.

349
00:31:38,560 --> 00:31:41,220
It was really about who was the better system.

350
00:31:41,340 --> 00:31:45,220
Was it the Soviet communist system or the American capitalist system?

351
00:31:45,221 --> 00:31:48,900
And both countries viewed the space program as their crowning achievements,

352
00:31:49,420 --> 00:31:53,640
as the pinnacle of their achievements both sociologically and technologically as nations.

353
00:31:54,280 --> 00:31:55,228
At 9.28 p.m.

354
00:31:55,229 --> 00:32:02,100
on June 29th, the cosmonauts unducked Soyuz 11 and drifted free from the space station.

355
00:32:03,640 --> 00:32:09,920
In accordance with procedure, they changed from full-pressure suits into space-saving woolen flight suits.

356
00:32:09,921 --> 00:32:17,920
The cosmonauts, when they got out of the space station, would leave their suits up there because they couldn't afford the weight.

357
00:32:17,921 --> 00:32:20,499
They said, fine, we won't have pressure suits on.

358
00:32:20,500 --> 00:32:23,920
We've got a nice pressurized cabin, don't have to worry about it.

359
00:32:23,921 --> 00:32:30,920
The flawless recovery was tracked by the Soviets as the cosmonauts descended through Earth's atmosphere.

360
00:32:30,921 --> 00:32:37,920
Unlike American astronauts who splashed down in the ocean, cosmonauts touched down on land.

361
00:32:37,921 --> 00:32:44,920
When recovery crews reached the descent vehicle, they were expecting to greet three jubilant heroes.

362
00:32:51,920 --> 00:32:54,920
But the cosmonauts were dead.

363
00:32:54,921 --> 00:33:00,920
The stunned ground crew attempted artificial respiration, but it was too late.

364
00:33:01,920 --> 00:33:06,920
The tragedy was a black mark on the Soviet space program.

365
00:33:07,920 --> 00:33:12,920
The mournful march of a state funeral echoed through Red Square.

366
00:33:12,921 --> 00:33:19,920
Scientists speculated about what happened and what potential blow it would deal to the future of manned space flight.

367
00:33:19,921 --> 00:33:24,920
There were theories that perhaps it was just too long for these cosmonauts to be in space.

368
00:33:24,921 --> 00:33:29,920
24 days is too long. Space is dangerous. Maybe their hearts couldn't take it.

369
00:33:29,921 --> 00:33:34,920
These spacecraft are the only thing between these people and the vacuum of space.

370
00:33:34,921 --> 00:33:38,220
There were some ideas that some chemicals, some noxious chemicals,

371
00:33:38,400 --> 00:33:40,960
had leaked out of the spacecraft and poisoned the astronauts.

372
00:33:41,980 --> 00:33:48,180
A Soviet commission investigating the tragedy reported that a rapid drop in cockpit pressure occurred

373
00:33:48,181 --> 00:33:51,740
because of a flaw in the integrity of the cabin seal.

374
00:33:54,620 --> 00:33:59,660
As far as NASA was concerned, the initial report only raised more questions.

375
00:33:59,661 --> 00:34:04,660
The Americans needed hard information because in the near future,

376
00:34:04,661 --> 00:34:10,660
their astronauts were to occupy a Soviet spacecraft as part of a joint space venture.

377
00:34:10,661 --> 00:34:15,660
American experts really wanted to understand what happened to these three cosmonauts.

378
00:34:15,661 --> 00:34:18,660
Could it happen to American astronauts?

379
00:34:18,661 --> 00:34:21,660
Could the Apollo astronauts suffer a similar fate?

380
00:34:21,661 --> 00:34:26,660
And so there were some very high-level contacts between American and Soviet politicians

381
00:34:26,661 --> 00:34:33,660
politicians asking for the Soviets to share information, which the Soviets did.

382
00:34:33,661 --> 00:34:43,660
A Soviet-American collaboration uncovered the truth by meticulously reconstructing the final fatal moments of Soyuz 11.

383
00:34:43,661 --> 00:34:52,660
The problem was traced to a design flaw in the way the Soyuz spacecraft separated from the Soyuz space station.

384
00:34:52,661 --> 00:35:00,720
To send the cosmonauts on their way home, their capsule was freed from the space station when a set of explosive bolts fired one by one.

385
00:35:01,780 --> 00:35:07,520
But unfortunately, on the flight of Soyuz 11, the explosive bolts fired all at once.

386
00:35:08,660 --> 00:35:12,140
Severe vibrations caused a pressure equalization valve to open.

387
00:35:13,280 --> 00:35:22,500
The three cosmonauts not only suffocated, but as the air pressure dropped rapidly in the cabin, nitrogen present in their tissues was released.

388
00:35:22,660 --> 00:35:25,260
creating gas bubbles.

389
00:35:25,261 --> 00:35:30,260
This phenomenon, common to deep sea divers, is called the bends.

390
00:35:30,261 --> 00:35:35,260
Once these bubbles form in the brain, spinal cord, or even peripheral nerves,

391
00:35:35,261 --> 00:35:38,560
paralysis and convulsions are the result.

392
00:35:38,561 --> 00:35:46,260
And in the cosmonaut's tragic case, they succumb to a quick and brutal death.

393
00:35:46,261 --> 00:35:50,260
Their blood went hypoxic, which means that, among other things,

394
00:35:50,261 --> 00:35:54,940
the nitrogen came out of solution in the blood, and their blood, you could say, essentially boiled.

395
00:35:55,400 --> 00:36:00,340
It would be like going from the bottom of the ocean to the surface in a fraction of a second.

396
00:36:01,020 --> 00:36:06,120
So their death was extremely rapid and, by all accounts, painless.

397
00:36:07,980 --> 00:36:11,380
Upon completion of the joint Soviet-American investigation,

398
00:36:12,220 --> 00:36:16,140
NASA was reassured that since their spacecraft hatch was designed differently,

399
00:36:16,141 --> 00:36:23,140
A similar accident could not happen during an Apollo mission.

400
00:36:25,740 --> 00:36:32,740
While tragic, the Soyuz 11 mission marked the beginning of a dialogue between Soviet and American scientists

401
00:36:32,741 --> 00:36:39,740
that would serve both well in future space station missions.

402
00:36:39,741 --> 00:36:50,740
Next, a search for life in outer space has unexpected consequences.

403
00:36:50,741 --> 00:36:55,740
The record to date for the longest stay in space is held by cosmonaut Sergey Abdiyev,

404
00:36:55,741 --> 00:37:00,740
who remained aboard Mir for 742 days.

405
00:37:00,741 --> 00:37:04,740
Engineering Disasters 3 will return on Modern Marvels.

406
00:37:09,740 --> 00:37:16,820
On December 11, 1998, NASA launched a Delta II rocket with precious payload aboard.

407
00:37:18,660 --> 00:37:22,460
The Mars Climate Orbiter was on its way to the Red Planet.

408
00:37:24,080 --> 00:37:30,400
Two instruments aboard the spacecraft, the pressure infrared radiometer and the Mars color imager,

409
00:37:30,880 --> 00:37:34,080
would provide the first detailed data about the Martian atmosphere.

410
00:37:34,081 --> 00:37:41,080
The orbiter would remain in Martian orbit for two years and function as a relay station

411
00:37:41,081 --> 00:37:44,080
for the next planned mission.

412
00:37:44,081 --> 00:37:51,080
In this way, NASA would fly two smaller, cheaper missions rather than a single costly mission.

413
00:37:51,081 --> 00:37:58,080
It was a two-for-one approach that was hailed for its economy and efficiency.

414
00:37:58,081 --> 00:38:03,080
NASA engineers had adopted a philosophy they called Faster, Better, Cheaper,

415
00:38:03,081 --> 00:38:10,080
believing that it would allow them to fly more missions and spend less.

416
00:38:10,081 --> 00:38:17,080
On September 23, 1999, at 2.01 a.m. Pacific Daylight Time,

417
00:38:17,081 --> 00:38:21,080
the probe was supposed to fire its main engine,

418
00:38:21,081 --> 00:38:24,080
pass behind the Red Planet,

419
00:38:24,081 --> 00:38:31,080
then enter an orbit 93 miles above the Martian surface.

420
00:38:31,081 --> 00:38:37,400
But instead, when it should have reemerged from behind Mars and reestablished radio contact from orbit,

421
00:38:38,040 --> 00:38:40,340
there was no radio signal.

422
00:38:42,780 --> 00:38:49,360
Scientists later concluded that it went in far too low, only 37 miles above the surface.

423
00:38:50,100 --> 00:38:56,780
The cause was a grade school level calculation error, and the effect, an engineering disaster.

424
00:38:56,781 --> 00:39:01,880
What happened was that the engines ended up burning too long,

425
00:39:01,881 --> 00:39:07,020
and the spacecraft went too low into the atmosphere, slowed down too much,

426
00:39:07,380 --> 00:39:12,440
dived into the atmosphere, and burned up in the atmosphere as far as we could tell.

427
00:39:13,780 --> 00:39:16,000
It was an embarrassing blunder.

428
00:39:16,960 --> 00:39:20,000
The spacecraft's builders, Lockheed Martin Astronautics,

429
00:39:20,440 --> 00:39:25,700
calculated trajectory and engine thrust using the English system of inches, feet, and pounds.

430
00:39:26,780 --> 00:39:34,380
But NASA's navigation team assumed those numbers were metric, millimeters, meters, and newtons.

431
00:39:34,780 --> 00:39:40,820
They entered the wrong values into the navigation computer, sending the climate orbiter off course.

432
00:39:42,160 --> 00:39:48,260
When the spacecraft entered the Martian atmosphere at 10,000 miles per hour, it was doomed.

433
00:39:49,200 --> 00:39:53,380
NASA had to answer for the loss of the $125 million vehicle.

434
00:39:53,381 --> 00:39:58,380
The navigation team came on the program two months before the launch.

435
00:39:58,381 --> 00:40:01,380
So they were not familiar. They hadn't attended the preliminary design review.

436
00:40:01,381 --> 00:40:03,380
They hadn't attended the critical design review.

437
00:40:03,381 --> 00:40:06,380
They were really not familiar with the spacecraft.

438
00:40:06,381 --> 00:40:12,380
Although the Mars Climate Orbiter failed, NASA had already committed to its next Mars mission.

439
00:40:12,381 --> 00:40:13,315
mission.

440
00:40:13,316 --> 00:40:22,660
On December 3, 1999, the $165 million Mars polar lander was to touch down on the

441
00:40:22,661 --> 00:40:26,987
Martian South Pole. Its mission, a search for water.

442
00:40:26,988 --> 00:40:29,900
Scientists hypothesized that water,

443
00:40:30,400 --> 00:40:36,160
vital to life as we know it, once flowed across the Martian landscape and could be stored

444
00:40:36,161 --> 00:40:37,500
as ice at the pole.

445
00:40:40,100 --> 00:40:45,600
One of the exciting parts of the Mars Polar Lander payload was something called the TIGA,

446
00:40:46,100 --> 00:40:48,700
which was the Thermal Evolved Gas Analyzer.

447
00:40:48,960 --> 00:40:53,620
There was a robot arm on the Lander which would scoop up some soil, put it into the

448
00:40:53,621 --> 00:40:58,080
TIGA instrument, and then there was an oven that would heat it up and measure the various

449
00:40:58,081 --> 00:41:00,720
components of the gases as they came off.

450
00:41:00,721 --> 00:41:06,860
And so it was going to detect not only how much water there was, but maybe the isotopic composition,

451
00:41:07,020 --> 00:41:10,760
that is, how many neutrons were there in that particular kind of water.

452
00:41:11,500 --> 00:41:14,020
Still stinging from the Mars orbiter debacle,

453
00:41:14,680 --> 00:41:18,460
jet propulsion lab engineers knew the stakes were higher this time.

454
00:41:19,920 --> 00:41:24,420
Not only did the polar lander cost $40 million more than the Mars orbiter,

455
00:41:25,000 --> 00:41:28,000
but landing on Mars was more difficult than orbiting it.

456
00:41:28,001 --> 00:41:36,740
Landing on another planet is probably the most difficult thing we do, and every detail must be right.

457
00:41:37,520 --> 00:41:43,360
So we must learn, and we must learn quickly from the loss of Mars Climate Orbiter.

458
00:41:44,220 --> 00:41:49,760
Anxious engineers double-checked their mission plan and made design modifications to the spacecraft.

459
00:41:49,761 --> 00:41:59,260
They indeed discovered that there was a possibility that the rockets wouldn't start properly after having been in the cold of space for a long time.

460
00:41:59,500 --> 00:42:05,980
So they did a bunch of tests and verified that indeed if they used certain procedures that it would be fine.

461
00:42:06,900 --> 00:42:07,889
At 12.39 p.m.

462
00:42:07,890 --> 00:42:13,820
on December 3rd, NASA was expecting to receive a signal from the polar lander.

463
00:42:15,320 --> 00:42:17,400
But no signal ever came.

464
00:42:17,401 --> 00:42:21,400
The spacecraft was never heard from again.

465
00:42:21,401 --> 00:42:27,400
Scientists believe that like the Climate Orbiter, the Polar Lander crashed on the Martian surface,

466
00:42:27,401 --> 00:42:31,400
but this time for a different reason.

467
00:42:31,401 --> 00:42:36,400
A software error caused the Polar Lander to deploy its landing legs too early

468
00:42:36,401 --> 00:42:41,400
and turn off its engines more than 100 feet from the planet's surface.

469
00:42:41,401 --> 00:42:43,400
And the whole thing crashed.

470
00:42:43,401 --> 00:42:49,520
Well, it was definitely a bad missed opportunity because that was their opportunity to get to the South Pole.

471
00:42:49,640 --> 00:42:53,600
And if there's water anywhere near the surface on Mars, it's at the poles.

472
00:42:54,040 --> 00:42:57,680
And so this was going to be the experiment that could really go look for water.

473
00:42:59,020 --> 00:43:02,680
NASA had egg on its face for the second time in as many months.

474
00:43:04,240 --> 00:43:10,080
Failure analysis revealed the problem to be the very philosophy of faster, better, cheaper

475
00:43:10,081 --> 00:43:13,180
that NASA adopted to streamline its operations.

476
00:43:13,400 --> 00:43:20,260
The philosophy was, well, hey, if we send small missions frequently, then we're spreading the risk.

477
00:43:20,780 --> 00:43:25,940
And on top of that, we can take advantage of what we learn with one mission to design another mission.

478
00:43:26,520 --> 00:43:32,500
Unfortunately, that works rather well as long as your appetite doesn't exceed your resources.

479
00:43:33,540 --> 00:43:40,400
Following the twin disasters of the Climate Orbiter and Polar Lander, NASA cut back on its missions to Mars.

480
00:43:41,360 --> 00:43:46,880
The next mission to search for water on the Red Planet may not come until 2007.

481
00:43:49,260 --> 00:43:52,740
Hard lessons came from each of these engineering disasters.

482
00:43:53,220 --> 00:43:56,420
When building the tallest tower on land or at sea,

483
00:43:57,140 --> 00:43:59,920
exploring interplanetary or subatomic space,

484
00:44:00,440 --> 00:44:03,120
engineers and designers went out on a limb

485
00:44:03,121 --> 00:44:07,380
and unfortunately found their reach exceeded their grasp.

486
00:44:07,381 --> 00:44:14,780
In ambitious projects like these, necessity was the mother of invention, but also midwife to mistakes.

487
00:44:15,860 --> 00:44:20,360
Minor errors led to complicated problems and even cost lives.

488
00:44:21,280 --> 00:44:26,460
Perhaps an insatiable desire to push to the limit is hardwired into our genetic code,

489
00:44:26,900 --> 00:44:32,100
but going to the maximum in extreme situations will always demand its due.

490
00:44:32,101 --> 00:44:39,100
Tonight on the History Channel, it wasn't just about riding the range.

491
00:44:39,320 --> 00:44:42,300
You had to know how to strut your stuff for an audience and a camera.

492
00:44:42,400 --> 00:44:45,300
You're always doing a part that you know John Wayne could have done better.

493
00:44:45,301 --> 00:44:50,360
We're talking about cowboys in action in Wild West shows, the Western movies, and TV.

494
00:44:50,860 --> 00:44:52,240
When cowboys were king.

495
00:44:52,560 --> 00:44:54,380
Tonight at 8 on the History Channel.

